epairing commit. The contract covers ledger and snapshot inputs produced by supported signing and act routes; hand-edited carriers, alias spellings and sub-second timing windows are outside it.\"\n+detail = \"When an amendment-record walk returns records, the trunk mint excludes only a record whose id is re-attested by an act-ledger entry added within the requested range, whose whole approved and routed traced cell sets at the range's after revision equal that row's approved-snapshot copy at that revision, and for which either no entry the range added after the latest re-attesting one records a copy of that row's registry or omits its copy record (a legacy entry, whose copy set is unknown), or the row's routed traced cells at the after revision equal those at the before revision. An owner row carries a held-MEANING record out of the amendment row. A later act copying the registry without re-attesting the row cannot have absorbed an unattested change to its approved cells, only to its routed traced cells, so the first arm proves the copy is the re-attesting act's own and the second proves the row's routed traced cells are the same at the range's two ends, whether the range is one squashed commit or many. The second arm's one limit: a routed traced cell moved before the re-attesting act and restored after it is re-anchored by a later act's copy of the registry, as any traced refresh is, and the row settles. A row re-attested in the range whose routed traced cells moved in the range is not proven settled when a later act copies its registry, so it is minted even when its own act judged it. The comparison includes cells outside the net range diff. A tree that does not list the ledger supplies no acts, and a missing copy or a copy without the row leaves the record to mint. A listed ledger or snapshot copy that cannot be read or does not parse refuses the mint by naming the carrier and revision; it is never treated as absent. The same filtering result serves the post-merge and range-sweep routes. Its refusal states that the mint was refused while the merge stands and directs recovery through a fetch followed by a sweep over the same range, or through a reviewed repair followed by a sweep whose after revision is the repairing commit. The contract covers ledger and snapshot inputs produced by supported signing and act routes; hand-edited carriers, alias spellings and sub-second timing windows are outside it.\"\n rationale = \"Suppressing a record without both a new re-attestation and equality to its approved copy would hide a later amendment; treating a present unreadable carrier as absence would make the suppression decision unauditable. The later-writer arm accepts one bound rather than minting on every later copy of the registry: a routed traced cell restored between two acts is re-anchored by the later copy, as the snapshot already re-anchors any traced change without a verdict.\"\n test_refs = \"(see TC-403)\"\n status = \"Approved\"\n@@ -3215,7 +3215,7 @@ sr_refs = [\"SR-148\"]\n title = \"Pending open-item citation integrity\"\n module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py;project-trajectory/scripts/kitlib/spine.py\"\n code_symbol = \"\"\"uncited_open_item_findings/open_item_specref_findings/_registry_ref/_registry_ref_findings/_specref_staleness/open_item_queue/open_items_at/_held_rows\"\"\"\n-detail = \"\"\"Report as an error, whatever the gate and before the checker's no-work-items return, a pending open item that no queued work item cites in its needs, read from the one queue projection, open_item_queue, that also feeds the owner surface and the status snapshot; a citer that is not queued does not count. For each open work row whose SpecRef names the open-items registry under either carrier's spelling, report the row when the SpecRef carries an #OI anchor naming an item its needs do not cite, and when none of the items it cites is pending or absent from the registry, a row citing none included. Backlog staleness clocks such a SpecRef per item, each item the row cites and the item its anchor names, never by the registry file, so filing another item stales no row.\"\"\"\n+detail = \"\"\"Report as an error, whatever the gate and before the checker's no-work-items return, a pending open item that no queued or claimed work item cites in its needs, read from the one queue projection, open_item_queue, that also feeds the owner surface and the status snapshot; a citer that is neither queued nor claimed does not count. For each open work row whose SpecRef names the open-items registry under either carrier's spelling, report the row when the SpecRef carries an #OI anchor naming an item its needs do not cite, and when none of the items it cites is pending or absent from the registry, a row citing none included. Backlog staleness clocks such a SpecRef per item, each item the row cites and the item its anchor names, never by the registry file, so filing another item stales no row.\"\"\"\n rationale = \"\"\"A pending owner decision must remain visible beside the work it holds, while a ruled item is no longer the specification the released work implements. Clocking a registry SpecRef by the registry file would stale every placeholder whenever any decision is filed, so the clock reads the items the row cites.\"\"\"\n test_refs = \"TC-314\"\n status = \"Approved\"\ndiff --git a/docs/test/test-cases.toml b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\nindex 110771c85..bc0612356 100644\n--- a/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n+++ b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n@@ -1202,12 +1202,12 @@ phase = 3\n [test.TC-123]\n verifies = [\"SR-049\", \"LLR-118\"]\n level = \"Unit\"\n-method = \"\"\"Drive gen_open_items over temp repos: assert a pending registry row a queued work item cites renders as a brief beside the rows citing it and a RULED row does not; that a pending row no queued work item cites is not a card but is named in an integrity notice linking its registry record, and that the page claims an empty queue only when neither a card nor a notice remains; that the status snapshot's open-items and Blocked lists name the same cited and uncited items as the page; that Drafted spine rows and approved rows drifted from the approved snapshot both surface (approval owed vs re-attest owed) while an undrifted Approved row does not; that a section with no changed cells says what is true — no cell differs from the approved snapshot, the row's own Status asking for a human — and never CHECK THE BASELINE nor nothing-changed; that --check bites on drift as a plain regenerate-and-compare, with no baseline stamp left in the view to re-read; that the whole thing is vacuous with neither registry nor view; that registry prose is HTML-escaped; that the word diff marks only what moved and the percentage counts words not whitespace; that the theme tokens equal the dashboard's emitted values (a drift guard, not an extraction); and that Decisions to review is the page's last section. It also drives verdict_reattest_block: every act-ledger entry naming a verdict is listed newest first with its re-attested rows and its verdict file, an entry naming none is left out, a ledger with no such entry reads None recorded, and the rendered page carries the list.\"\"\"\n+method = \"\"\"Drive gen_open_items over temp repos: assert a pending registry row a queued or claimed work item cites renders as a brief beside the rows citing it and a RULED row does not; that a pending row no queued or claimed work item cites is not a card but is named in an integrity notice linking its registry record, and that the page claims an empty queue only when neither a card nor a notice remains; that the status snapshot's open-items and Blocked lists name the same cited and uncited items as the page, including a claimed citer's card and Blocked line; that Drafted spine rows and approved rows drifted from the approved snapshot both surface (approval owed vs re-attest owed) while an undrifted Approved row does not; that a section with no changed cells says what is true — no cell differs from the approved snapshot, the row's own Status asking for a human — and never CHECK THE BASELINE nor nothing-changed; that --check bites on drift as a plain regenerate-and-compare, with no baseline stamp left in the view to re-read; that the whole thing is vacuous with neither registry nor view; that registry prose is HTML-escaped; that the word diff marks only what moved and the percentage counts words not whitespace; that the theme tokens equal the dashboard's emitted values (a drift guard, not an extraction); and that Decisions to review is the page's last section. It also drives verdict_reattest_block: every act-ledger entry naming a verdict is listed newest first with its re-attested rows and its verdict file, an entry naming none is left out, a ledger with no such entry reads None recorded, and the rendered page carries the list.\"\"\"\n tier = \"Full\"\n parameters = \"tests/test_gen_open_items.py (temp repos; one git-backed fixture for the baseline paths)\"\n expected = \"\"\"Every assertion holds, each with its negative half.\"\"\"\n automated = \"Yes\"\n-evidence = \"\"\"tests/test_gen_open_items.py; tests/test_gen_open_items_render.py::test_a_verdict_reattestation_stays_listed_on_the_owner_surface; tests/test_gen_open_items_render.py::test_the_verdict_audit_list_is_newest_first; tests/test_open_item_queue.py\"\"\"\n+evidence = \"\"\"tests/test_gen_open_items.py; tests/test_gen_open_items_render.py::test_a_verdict_reattestation_stays_listed_on_the_owner_surface; tests/test_gen_open_items_render.py::test_the_verdict_audit_list_is_newest_first; tests/test_open_item_queue.py; tests/test_open_item_readiness.py::test_a_claimed_citer_is_a_card_and_on_the_blocked_list\"\"\"\n status = \"Approved\"\n phase = 4\n \n@@ -1456,13 +1456,13 @@ status = \"Approved\"\n phase = 4\n \n [test.TC-147]\n-verifies = [\"SR-174\", \"SR-228\", \"SR-148\", \"LLR-153\", \"IF-091\"]\n+verifies = [\"SR-174\", \"SR-228\", \"SR-148\", \"LLR-153\", \"IF-091\", \"IF-224\"]\n level = \"Integration\"\n-method = \"\"\"Run the intake suite against real git repos, red-then-green per trigger (trigger (b) keys on the close's immutable REPORT PATH, so a re-sweep dedupes exactly and a genuinely second close is a second row): an approved SR amendment mints ONE adjudication row (id max+1, the before/after listing in ## Context) and an LLR SR-Refs re-point mints while a Module-only move stays silent (the ruled routing); a merged handback mints the disposition row (NEEDS-HUMAN reason routes strong; a handed-back adjudication row mints NOTHING and its own handback attempt is refused structurally); a merged adjudication row's ## Dispositions drafts mint at its merge with planmode=dual passing through kind-derived, and a malformed block, an unknown key, a second adjudication kind or a bad bar value refuse with nothing minted; a draft carrying an [open_item] table mints a pending open item with no work-item pointer and a queued successor whose needs cite it and which reads blocked, the successor keeping its own SpecRef and a draft with none getting the item's registry record; the census mints gap rows once and dedupes on re-run; every re-run is idempotent by exact-title dedup; the context block renders every join in failure-cost order, a pending open item the row's kin cite in needs among them while one named only by a historical wi_refs is left out, and answers the empty string on a bare repo; a held dial recommends and leaves the registries byte-identical; a released dial writes nothing, refusing by name a row not already Approved and skipping one that is; an unreadable dial or stage reads as held; adjudication_action returns flip released, reattest held with a CLARITY verdict, recommend otherwise; and an amended approved need, assumption or surrogate mints one amendment row. An adopts-only block allocates no id; a drafted title resolves its identity and lineage together, refusing a title match on another lineage before any write and retaining one on the draft's own lineage; and a replay re-points a late dependent to the full successor set, drafts and adoptions together, committing nothing when nothing is left to re-point.\"\"\"\n+method = \"\"\"Run the intake suite against real git repos, red-then-green per trigger (trigger (b) keys on the close's immutable REPORT PATH, so a re-sweep dedupes exactly and a genuinely second close is a second row): an approved SR amendment mints ONE adjudication row (id max+1, the before/after listing in ## Context) and an LLR SR-Refs re-point mints while a Module-only move stays silent (the ruled routing); a merged handback mints the disposition row (NEEDS-HUMAN reason routes strong; a handed-back adjudication row mints NOTHING and its own handback attempt is refused structurally); a merged adjudication row's ## Dispositions drafts mint at its merge with planmode=dual passing through kind-derived, and a malformed block, an unknown key, a second adjudication kind or a bad bar value refuse with nothing minted; a draft carrying an [open_item] table mints a pending open item with no work-item pointer and a queued successor whose needs cite it and which reads blocked, the successor keeping its own SpecRef and a draft with none getting the item's registry record; the census mints gap rows once and dedupes on re-run; every re-run is idempotent by exact-title dedup; an accepted held-rung MEANING verdict whose row differs from its approved-snapshot copy mints one queued owner row and one pending item per verdict, whether the verdict is blessed or not, while CLARITY, a released rung, equality to the copy, an unaccepted verdict, and replay mint nothing; the latest accepted verdict decides a row sat twice, and rows the owner row carries leave the amendment row; the context block renders every join in failure-cost order, a pending open item the row's kin cite in needs among them while one named only by a historical wi_refs is left out, and answers the empty string on a bare repo; a held dial recommends and leaves the registries byte-identical; a released dial writes nothing, refusing by name a row not already Approved and skipping one that is; an unreadable dial or stage reads as held; adjudication_action returns flip released, reattest held with a CLARITY verdict, recommend otherwise; and an amended approved need, assumption or surrogate mints one amendment row. An adopts-only block allocates no id; a drafted title resolves its identity and lineage together, refusing a title match on another lineage before any write and retaining one on the draft's own lineage; and a replay re-points a late dependent to the full successor set, drafts and adoptions together, committing nothing when nothing is left to re-point.\"\"\"\n tier = \"Full\"\n expected = \"Satisfies SR-174 AcceptanceCriteria via the LLR-153 detail contract; an adopts-only block allocates no id, title identity and lineage resolve together, and replay re-points the full successor set under LLR-153's replay clause and LLR-335's single-lineage operation.\"\n automated = \"Yes\"\n-evidence = \"\"\"tests/test_intake.py; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_replay_re_points_a_late_dependent_to_the_full_successor_set; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_title_match_off_the_drafts_lineage_refuses_the_mint; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_drafts_title_match_on_its_own_lineage_stays_standing; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_replay_with_nothing_left_to_re_point_commits_nothing; tests/test_intake.py::test_drafts_and_adoptions_together_hold_the_full_successor_set; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_recorded_adoption_re_points_a_dependents_needs\"\"\"\n+evidence = \"\"\"tests/test_intake.py; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_replay_re_points_a_late_dependent_to_the_full_successor_set; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_title_match_off_the_drafts_lineage_refuses_the_mint; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_drafts_title_match_on_its_own_lineage_stays_standing; tests/test_intake.py::test_a_replay_with_nothing_left_to_re